# Tiny Habits

## Question 1
The most interesting idea for me was that even a very small habit, like doing one push-up, can lead to bigger changes over time. Starting small is better than trying to do everything at once.

## Question 2
B = MAP means Behavior = Motivation + Ability + Prompt.

- **M – Motivation:** Wanting to do something.
- **A – Ability:** How easy it is to do.
- **P – Prompt:** A reminder to do it.

We can build habits by making them small, easy and connecting them to our daily routine.

## Question 3
Celebrating after completing a habit makes us feel happy and motivates us to repeat it. Even a small smile or saying "Well done" helps build a positive connection with the habit.

## Question 4
The most interesting idea was that improving by just 1% every day can lead to big results over time. Small improvements made consistently can create a huge difference.

## Question 5
The book explains that habits help build our identity. Instead of saying "I want to become a reader," we should start thinking of ourselves as readers and prove it through small actions.

## Question 6
We can make habits easier by preparing things in advance, keeping necessary items nearby and starting with small actions. For example, keeping a book on the desk makes reading easier.

## Question 7
We can make bad habits harder by adding extra steps. For example, keeping our phones away while studying and turning off unnecessary notifications can reduce distractions.

## Question 8
I want to practise coding every day. I can keep my laptop ready, set a fixed time and start by solving at least one problem daily. Tracking my progress will help me stay consistent.

## Question 9
I want to reduce unnecessary social media usage. I can turn off notifications, set app limits and keep my phone away while working. This will help me avoid distractions and save time.
